<article class="env-remark" id="00ZU">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00ZU">Remark <span data-tag="00ZU">3.3.8.3</span>.</a> If $f: X \rightarrow S$ is a Kan fibration of simplicial sets, then every vertex $s \in S$ determines a Kan complex $X_{s} = \{ s\}  \times _{S} X$. One can think of the construction $s \mapsto X_{s}$ as supplying a map from $S$ to the “space” of all Kan complexes. Roughly speaking, one can think of Theorem <a data-tag="00ZS" href="/tag/00ZS">3.3.8.1</a> as asserting that this “space” itself behaves like a Kan complex. We will return to this idea in §<a data-tag="027M" href="/tag/027M">5.6</a>. </p>
</article>
